course: dictionary
lesson: 15463
title: Stirring: many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, so context helps you choose the right one. Stirring can mean mixing with a spoon, moving slightly, or making you feel strong emotion. Read the words around it to decide.
explain: «Stir the sugar into the water until it disappears.» Here stirring is a science or cooking action: moving a spoon round and round to mix. Stirring helps sugar dissolve faster in water.
explain: «Something was stirring in the bushes.» Nothing is being mixed here, so stirring means moving slightly, or starting to wake up. «The stirring speech made the whole crowd cheer.» Now stirring describes the speech itself: it makes you feel strong pride or excitement.
fact: Stirring can be a verb, an adjective, or a noun (a stirring).
fact: «Stirring» as a verb means moving a spoon or stick round and round in a liquid to mix it.
fact: «Stirring» as a verb can also mean moving slightly, or starting to wake up.
fact: «Stirring» as an adjective means making you feel strong emotion, like pride or excitement.
fact: «Stirring up trouble» is an informal phrase meaning trying to make people argue.
exercise: «The cook kept stirring the soup so the bits would not stick.» What is the cook doing? | Shouting at the pot | Sleeping in a chair | *Moving a spoon round and round to mix | Singing softly
exercise: «The stirring speech made the team cheer.» What does stirring mean here? | Falling asleep | *Making you feel strong pride or excitement | Making a loud, angry noise | Moving a spoon round a bowl
exercise: «Something was stirring under the leaves.» What does stirring mean here? | Mixing paint in a pot | Arguing with a friend | Being very cold | *Moving slightly, as if waking up
test: «Mia was stirring the sugar into her tea.» What does stirring mean? | *Moving a spoon round to mix | Shining brightly | Waking up slowly | Making people argue
test: «Ben was stirring up trouble at school again.» What does stirring up trouble mean? | Helping the teacher tidy up | Being very tired | *Trying to make people argue | Cleaning the classroom floor
exam: Which sentence uses stirring to mean moving slightly? | «The stirring speech was loud.» | «He was stirring up trouble.» | «The stirring soup smelled lovely.» | *«Something was stirring under the blanket.»
